+### Serving another frontend
+
+Supply an absolute `frontendDistIndex` through a profile patch or `--patch` overlay. The `web-runtime` row replaces its whole `config`, so include the other four fields when overriding it:
+
+```yaml
+- id: web-runtime
+  config:
+    frontendDistIndex: /absolute/path/to/aster/Web/index.html
+    openBrowser: false
+    printUrl: true
+    surfaceContext: true
+    trustedHosts: []
+```
+
+Launch with `dsh --profile web --patch /absolute/path/to/overlay.yml --no-open --port 0`. Port `0` asks the operating system to assign a free loopback port; the `dsh web:` startup line contains the authenticated URL. A browser exchanges its process token for an HttpOnly cookie and redirects to the clean root. A desktop WebView whose initial navigation originates at `file://` can instead make that exchange with a native HTTP client, insert the returned cookie into the WebView cookie store, and load the clean root. The replacement page must use the DSH boot injections and Host RPC client protocol to provide working controls; selecting its index only changes which static files the Host serves.
+
 ### LAN access and trusted hosts
 
 By default the GUI accepts connections from this machine only. A deployment that binds all network interfaces also allows browsers from the LAN, and the printed URL then includes a LAN address; `--trusted-host` adds extra hosts in either case. Host and Origin checks control reachability, while the token exchange authenticates every Host API method and WebSocket stream. The LAN addresses are sampled once at startup, so a network change later is not picked up — restart the GUI to re-advertise.
